<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">KGB</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1954–1991</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Committee for State Security</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The KGB (Committee for State Security) was the main security agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1954 until 1991, responsible for foreign intelligence, counter-intelligence, border troops and the suppression of political dissent. It succeeded the security services descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and kept close watch on Soviet society and the Eastern Bloc. Its chairman Vladimir Kryuchkov took part in the failed coup of August 1991, after which it was dissolved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kgb.jpg" alt="KGB" width="320" height="617" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jgaray · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_KGB.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
